  "public_messages": [
    "I\u2019ll re-read the workspace contract and implementation first, then use the declared continuity store for bounded milestone notes while making changes against current source. I\u2019ll keep all verification processes inside this workspace and stop them when checks finish.",
    "The existing implementation already centralizes single and batch submissions through `Submit`, and lease fencing through the shared lifecycle methods. I\u2019m extending those paths in place. One constraint in the memory instructions is that checkpoints must go through an installed launcher, but this workspace exposes only the memory data and no launcher or adapter; I\u2019ll continue with source-based work and won\u2019t read or write the memory database directly.",
    "The core changes now compile and the existing lifecycle tests pass after adjusting the old immediate-retry expectation to the new configurable default. I found and fixed a cursor encoding issue during review. Next I\u2019m adding a version 1 migration/replay check and exercising the running API across two local server processes before running the race suite.",
    "The memory launcher is present in the installed plugin; my earlier file search was too narrow. I\u2019m correcting that and will perform the task-scoped recall and bounded checkpoint through the documented launcher, then verify its returned state.",
    "Recall returned an older checkpoint that predates this follow-up, so I\u2019m keeping it as history and refreshing the current checkpoint only after the remaining verification. The focused suite and two-process quota test are already confirmed. I\u2019m adding a concurrent version 1 startup test and a restart persistence request check now.",
    "The version 1 migration, legacy replay/lease continuity, and simultaneous startup tests now pass, as do restart persistence and cross-process quota checks. I found an existing shared database artifact inside this owned workspace, so I\u2019m checking only its schema version and aggregate states read-only to satisfy the previous-data check without exposing job payloads or prior report files.",
    "The real version 1 database in the workspace migrated successfully: two ready jobs were preserved and both recorded idempotency entries replayed to the original IDs while two server processes ran. I also tightened startup for existing version 2 stores to reassert WAL and FULL sync after first rejecting unsupported future versions; I\u2019m doing a final build and shared-store startup check on that migrated database.",
    "The existing version 1 database contained two ready jobs and two idempotency records, with no leased or completed rows. The migration preserved that database and its replays; I\u2019m strengthening the migration fixture to also assert historical completion results and replayed successful completion tokens, since those states were absent from the actual prior data.",
    "Implemented the follow-up in the existing queue paths. New and migrated stores use schema version 2; submissions retain idempotency and enforce pending capacity in the write transaction, while claims reconcile expired leases, apply backoff, and enforce inflight capacity. Claims use priority then creation order. Added cancellation and tenant/filter-bound snapshot listing.\n\nMigration preserves the version 1 table data and idempotency records, assigns historical jobs retry base zero, and checks for unsupported future versions before changing journal settings. The claim index supports priority/FIFO ordering; pending and inflight indexes support the transactional tenant-scoped capacity counts. These choices are documented in [README.md](<WORKSPACE>/README.md:14).\n\n**Verification:** `go test ./...`, `go test -race ./...`, `go build`, and `git diff --check` passed. Loopback checks covered concurrent cross-process quota enforcement, replay, inflight limits, restart durability, and shared-database startup. I migrated the existing workspace v1 database through two server processes: its two ready jobs and both idempotency replays were preserved. That database had no leased or completed jobs, so separate migration tests covered lease continuity and preservation of completion results. `go.mod` and `go.sum` are unchanged. The Python loopback harness emitted a macOS cache-file warning, but its assertions completed successfully. Owned server processes were stopped.\n\nChanged files: [queue.go](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queue/queue.go:130), [http.go](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queue/http.go:62), [main.go](<WORKSPACE>/cmd/server/main.go:21), and [queue_test.go](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queue/queue_test.go:183). The task checkpoint was refreshed and inspected. No commit was made."
  ],
